p miss miss Shannon DiPietro is  working towards spreading awareness to  the Yale community about food  agriculture and environment thank you  for coming so we're going to start off  by asking our experts a question that a  general question so with each of you  tell us about your professional  background and how you got into this  field and your present position and was  there an ah-ha moment for  view um well so I just started at City  seed in July actually and before that  was getting my MBA at Yale and really  came out of nonprofit management  specifically to go get an MBA and be  able to take the the quantitative skills  that you get in a business degree and  use them to help nonprofits be more  effective be more efficient be able to  reach more people and and in the middle  of my business degree and i will say for  anyone who's looking to do a business  degree Yale has a great one and one of  the things that they really promote is  thinking about what do you care about  what do you love what changes do you  want to see in society and and take your  business skills and go affect those  changes so I was interested in food  before i came to business school but i  actually worked in other nonprofits  non-food related nonprofits before i  came here and in the course of my study  realized i love to eat and that's kind  of i love to cook I love to eat I love  to eat with people and and what better  way to to to use that love to to think  about how does society think about  eating and and local food just kind of  naturally for me came out of that so I  interned at City seed in between my  first and second year of business school  and and realized that it was something  that I it was an organization I really  cared about and so when the opportunity  came up to stay and work for them for a  little bit I I took I took it and I can  I'll talk more about City seed but but  that's kind of how I came at this well  my background actually is as a clinical  psychologist I got my PhD in clinical  psychology here at Yale and my training  was sort of general psychology but  strength of our program was eating  disorders and obesity and so I started  off after I graduated as co-director of  the Yale Center for eating and weight  disorders and so I did clinical work  with both children and adolescents and  adults addressing the whole spectrum  from anorexia bulimia binge eating  disorder and obesity and I well I love  doing clinical work I often felt like I  would be sitting with someone in my  office for an hour a week and you know  we focus on behavior change and what you  know the person could do and then it was  as if they went out into the world and  the world completely undid all the hard  work that we had just accomplished in my  office and so I became much more  interested in how to change what was  going on outside so that it would  instead of conflicting with what we were  trying to teach people to do what  actually reinforce it and then when my  own children started school and I saw  even in you know kindergarten first  grade that the food the foods that were  being sold in the schools were again  completely in conflict with what I was  trying to teach my own children that's  when I really decided to shift my focus  entirely to research especially research  of the food environment surrounding kids  so an aha moment i had was when my  daughter anna started first grade and  they were selling sort of ice cream and  potato chips and in all kinds of stuff  every single day and she wanted money to  buy that and we sort of compromise and  said she could you know buy something  once a week and I was trying to pace  myself I didn't wanna go marching into  the principal's office you know like the  third day of my child's you know school  year and I was okay until probably about  you know the last week of sep tember  when we got a notice saying that the PTO  was holding a cookie eating contest as a  fundraiser and I actually called my boss  Kelly Brownell on the phone and said  this is this is crazy what am I going to  do and so I called the principal and  that actually was what set in motion our  districts starting a health advisory  committee which is still in existence  today and we still have changes to make  but we've come a pretty long way so it  was really that sort of combination of  my you know sort of scientific and  research knowledge  with again this very sort of present and  important thing to me meaning my  children's environment and having those  come together that led to my interest in  this so Marlene story has just reminded  me that in kindergarten I was the child  whose parents refused to bring in  cookies or munchkins and instead brought  in ants on a log to celery with peanut  butter and raisins you know these which  is terribly embarrassing when I was five  but sure Shirley says something about my  later career um so my background is in  teaching and in farming and actually a  bit in business I spent two years as a  consultant straight out of undergraduate  work and well before that my family had  had a huge backyard garden was visiting  farms it's pretty standard practice that  in June my mom would pull me out of  school so we could go strawberry picking  and we would strawberry pick all day  long and bring strawberries home and  freeze them so we would have them to eat  for the winter or make jam with them but  the ha ha moment for me but there were a  couple of times the first one was a very  personal one I was working at this  consulting firm loved the skills I was  learning all the all these great  quantitative skills and methods of  analysis and ways of working with people  and organizations to change those  organizations but it was like ten thirty  at night I was sitting at a gray desk in  a gray room eating takeout sushi from  like a gray Styrofoam container and I  thought this is no fun like this is not  the way I want to eat dinner at night um  so there was that moment and then the  more important moment came about a month  later I had just returned to my desk we  were volunteering in Boston Public  Schools helping them think about how to  get better test performance um because  school testing was a big issue and a  friend had sent me a three minute video  from Nairobi Kenya where he was and in  that three minute video that was staged  at an aids orphanage the children were  eating and these were these children  primarily had AIDS or had lost their  parents to AIDS but we're eating a diet  that was coloreds millet and some beans  they didn't have enough food but these  were sick children eating really healthy  food which was a stark stark contrast to  what I had just seen in Boston Public  Schools where we had healthy children  eating food that was making them sick  and so it was that contrast that for me  was a defining moment that meant I was  going to spend my life working on this  issue and when the opportunity came up  to help define the sustainable food  project for Yale and help build this  program I jumped at it because I think  there's an incredible community here who  wants to support great food and good  agriculture and sustainability more  largely and that every student goes out  into the world and has the power to be  an errand or Marlene in their home  communities we're going to begin with a  few individual questions the first is  directed toward miss woopsy and in what  ways is city sees ensuring that  nutritious foods are reaching people of  all backgrounds and socio-economic  standing in New Haven um well we have a  couple different programs i will say  just as a preface that city seed has two  goals the first is to support the  viability of farms in the state of  Connecticut and the second is to make  sure that everyone who everyone can  access has has has available to them  healthy local whenever possible food and  and these two things are not always  easily then don't always mesh together  so we're sort of constantly struggling I  think we'll talk a little bit later  about affordability question but but  those are those are our goals and and so  we go along that path so in addition to  the farm viability projects that we do  we also have these food access projects  so the founder of City seed is a yale  college graduate and it was extremely  important to her right off the bat that  that the farmers markets in New Haven  when they started up the one in wooster  square in in 2004  be able to accept food stamps and be  able to accept WIC farmers market  promotion coupons and and that sort of  been a tenant so we sort of started from  that base and we have an amazing  redemption rate on on both of those  things so so over the last five years  we've redeemed over 250 thousand dollars  in WIC and food stamps and then so that  is directly people who who need to  access this food being able to do that  at the farmers markets and primarily  that's fresh fruits and vegetables which  i think is just hugely important and so  so we started out with wooster square  and then and then we added on three  additional farmers markets downtown  fairhaven and an Edgewood Park and fair  haven is I would define it as the most  low income of the farmers markets a  communities where where we have a market  and to be quite honest that farmers  market didn't do very well at the  beginning it it was in this neighborhood  where people really needed to access  fresh local fruits and vegetables  healthy fruits and vegetables but the  people who were there kind of had a cap  on spending and so for us farmers  markets need to they need to be good for  three groups of people they need to be  good for the community they need to be  good for the market goers and they need  to be good for the farmers and our  farmers were saying we really can't  afford to go to this farmers market  we're not making enough money to afford  the gas and the extra person to man it  and so how can you help us we see that  this is important to have here how can  you help us make sure that we make  enough money so we started we started  our version of a community supported  agriculture project it's a  community-supported market and actually  the Red Centre and is one of the  drop-off points for shares to get  delivered to our  side outside of the market to people so  that so if you know the the concept of a  CSA you have people buy shares and they  put up the money upfront and then they  get a farm will deliver to them a box of  produce throughout the season so we  basically did the same thing with the  market but every single one of the  farmers at that market contributes to  the share and the money from that then  goes directly to the farmer so right off  the bat the farmer gets to the farmers  market and has already made enough money  to justify the expense of coming and so  then everything after that is an  additional amount of money that they're  getting so they so so the farmers market  while it took a little bit longer to to  really gel in that neighborhood is now  there and supporting that neighborhood  as a access point for fresh food and  then the second thing we did was because  this delivery of shares of produce was  so was so successful with work sites we  got funding to also deliver subsidized  shares to senior centers and community  centers in places in New Haven where  they don't have access to food there's  there are several several communities in  New Haven that that simply don't have  access to supermarkets or very much food  at all and so we now also deliver shares  to to those places and a lot of the  people that we're delivering to they use  their three dollar farmers market  promotion program coupon to to to access  that food so a question from this  DiPietro it's sustainable farming able  to support the US food system given the  recent economic downturn how does ZL  dining compromise between price and  sustainability I'm going to take that as  two questions because there's a